00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:10.560
crucial. The metric aggregation divergence problem in agent-based simulations and evolutionary

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:16.500
optimization. Here's the issue. Everyone talks about running thousands of simulations and using

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:21.720
evolutionary methods to find the best policies. But in practice, when your optimization algorithm,

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:26.860
tournament selection, and statistical validation layers each implement metric extraction independently,

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:31.040
you get what researchers are calling metric aggregation divergence.

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:37.260
Tiny differences in how you compute episode-level metrics, whether you use mean, max,

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:43.580
final value, or some custom aggregation, can create rank reversals that completely mislead your

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:48.500
entire optimization pipeline. Your champion might just be an artifact of how you happen to

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:55.240
average the numbers. The H-E-A-S approach solves this with a runtime-enforceable metric contract,

00:07:55.620 --> 00:08:00.340
essentially a single shared metrics episode callable that every stage in the pipeline uses

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:06.140
identically. In their experiments, this reduced rank reversals by 50% and produced a champion that

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:13.100
actually won all 32 held out scenarios. It also slashed the amount of coupling code from 160 lines

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:20.260
down to just five. The takeaway is clear if you're doing any serious evolutionary optimization or multi-objective

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:26.340
policy search with agents, enforce a uniform metric contract first. This is exactly the kind of

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:30.160
engineering discipline that separates production grade systems from weekend experiments.

